    {
      "key": "base",
      "name": "Base Case",
      "label": "base (no intervention)",
      "description": "AI capability follows calibrated SWE-bench sigmoid curve. No active government mitigation. Limited new job creation. Reflects current trajectory without major policy intervention.",
      "color": "#EF553B",
      "narrative": "Under the **Base Case**, AI capability follows the calibrated SWE-bench sigmoid with no policy course-correction. Unemployment median rises through the **late 2020s** and peaks at **25.4% in 2029**, with a P25–P75 band of **24.0–26.6%** and a P05–P95 envelope of **22.0–28.2%**.\n\n**Drivers.** Software-engineering automation drags the IT & Communications sector first (Mythos-style cascade), followed by administrative services and customer-facing roles. Sector substitution is partial — about 5% of at-risk jobs are saved by ad-hoc reskilling, and new-job creation contributes only ~0.1% of the 2025 base annually starting in 2030.\n\n**What it would take to avoid this.** A factor-of-5 increase in the new-jobs rate (i.e. closer to the Optimistic scenario's 0.6% / yr) and aggressive reskilling lifting mitigation from 5% to 30%+ would shave roughly 12 pp off the peak, bringing the trajectory close to the Optimistic envelope.",
